{"qid":"Q47690","names":["Babylonia"],"title":"Babylonia","endonym":{"native":"𒆳𒆍𒀭𒊏𒆠","lang":"akk"},"capsule":"Babylonia was a state centered on the lower Euphrates and Tigris plains of Mesopotamia (modern southern Iraq), taking its name from Babylon, which first rose to prominence under the Amorite First Dynasty founded by Sumu-abum c. 1894 BC. The dataset span of −1800 to −601 encompasses both the Old Babylonian apex under Hammurabi (r. 1792–1750 BC), whose law code and administrative correspondence are among the best-documented records of the ancient Near East, and the Kassite interlude (c. 1595–1155 BC), followed by successive Assyrian dominations. Babylonia's political identity was finally extinguished in 539 BC when Cyrus II of Persia entered Babylon without battle; the dataset's terminus of −601 means the Neo-Babylonian apogee under Nebuchadnezzar II falls outside the attested geometry.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Old Babylonian and Neo-Babylonian core territories are well attested from administrative and cadastral records; precise outer limits, especially to the east and north, are interpolated from campaign texts and are only moderately reliable.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Babylonia","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Babylonia"},{"label":"Livius.org: Babylon","url":"https://www.livius.org/articles/place/babylon/"}],"britannica":"place/Babylonia","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Babylonia","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Sumu-abum","nativeName":"𒀭𒋗𒈬𒀊𒁍𒌝","roman":"Sumu-abum","start":-1894,"end":-1881,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Sum